// Documented open effect-checker defect (defect batch 2, 2026-09-19, found while fixing the
// collection-bracket type defects). It sits alone in this directory because a compile rejection
// fails the whole directory.
//
// Defect: effects do not propagate through a call to a user function. silica-specification §9.3.1
// rule 4 ("Effect Propagation") spells out this exact program -- a helper whose sequence declares
// `proc[mem(normal)]`, called from a sequence that declares `proc[mem(normal)]` -- and requires the
// caller to declare the callee's effects. The checker instead reports the caller's declaration as
// unused. The same happens for device_io, and across module boundaries (a call into a collection
// module such as `wbt_set@empty`), which is why every trial in the tree that calls a collection
// module writes a bare `sequence` and why the collection-bracket trials of this batch had to drop
// their `proc[mem(normal)]` when they started compiling.
// Expected: 1, exit 0.
// Observed: E3010 "Unused effect declaration: mem(normal) - no function in sequence block requires
// this effect" at the caller's sequence (line 30, column 19).
//
// To run by hand:
//   mkdir -p /tmp/efp && cp defect_effect_propagation.silica /tmp/efp/ && cd /tmp/efp
//   echo defect_effect_propagation.silica > silica.config && /Volumes/2T/silica/binaries/silica-compiler

fn helper() -> List[int64, mem(normal)] {
    sequence proc[mem(normal)]
        xs: List[int64, mem(normal)] <- [1, 2]
    produces
        pure xs
    end
}

fn main() -> int64 {
    sequence proc[mem(normal), device_io]
        ys: List[int64, mem(normal)] <- helper();
        print_int64(ys.head);
        println("")
    produces
        pure 0
    end
}
